we take for granted in our lives choice  in every other area whether it's  pop-tarts or cereal or cellphones but  really education is the most important  thing especially as a parent you want  your kids to have a solid education  my name is Lisa Snell and I'm the  director of education for the reason  foundation and I've been working on  education and school choice issues for  about 20 years and probably the biggest  joy that I'm affiliated with school  choices I'm always trying to find good  choices for my own kids schools school  choice in the United States is really at  a tipping point in the sense that kids  in America have more choices today than  they've ever had before schools really  are no longer driven by where you live  in your address and residential  assignment instead you can choose a  school based on your interests and the  way that that happens is right now  there's 5,000 charter schools in the  United States serving more than 2  million kids and in some cities like Los  Angeles and New Orleans a much larger  number of kids get to go to those kinds  of schools there's also private  scholarship programs and tax credit  programs that allow you to take your  public school money to a private school  of your choice there's more than 35 of  those programs now in the United States  so across the board public schools have  because of the competition they have way  more magnet schools and they're getting  rid of residential assignments so  there's just a lot more choice it's much  better to be a kid if you want to choose  your school in 2011 number one public  schools are in for school choice because  they're now letting public dollars  follow kids to their school so public  schools have felt a lot of competition  from charter schools and school vouchers  and tax credits and school scholarships  and public schools generally have not  had school choice for every school in  their district and their financing  systems have been set up based on where  you live so you get to go to a school  based on residential assignment it's  your neighborhood school but public  schools because of competition have had  to change the way they do things by  opening up enrollment and letting the  money follow the child so even here in  California Governor Jerry Brown has just  come out with a proposal where he would  have a weighted student funding and the  state money would go where the child is  at based on the characteristics of the  individual child Governor mitch Daniels  and Indiana  just had a statewide school financing  program based on the money follow the  child and there's actually about 40  places now school districts like New  York City San Francisco Boston Chicago  where instead of having residential  assignment they actually let kids pick  the public school that they want to go  to and the district per pupil funding  follows that child to the school number  to across the country courts more and  more ruling that both vouchers and tax  credits are constitutional both state  and the Supreme Court nationally have  been siding with parents in terms  allowing them to use public dollars to  pick the school of their choice so in  April 2011 the Supreme Court voted for  example that tax credits could support  kids going to private schools because  the government never had the money in  the first place and they never were  public dollars in January of 2012  Indiana which just passed the largest  statewide voucher program in the nation  their state Supreme Court sided with  parents and said that that program was  constitutional and that it could be  expanded and kids could take vouchers in  Indiana to go to private schools  number three evidence shows that school  choice charter schools vouchers tax  credits works there's more and more  evidence that school choice works for  kids so for example in the city of New  Orleans now in 2012 about 90 percent of  kids are enrolled in charter schools and  for the last six years New Orleans has  seen very steady improvement in terms of  test scores for reading and math and  science and in terms of graduation rates  the kids are doing much better than  before they had choice and in addition  to that there were 20 studies that were  random assignment gold standard studies  that looked at the effect of vouchers  and school choice on public schools and  18 out of 19 found that public schools  perform better when they have  competition from vouchers and the 19th  study found really no difference every  empirical study in places like Milwaukee  Cleveland Florida  shows a positive  effect when it's a gold standard study  the ease randomized assignments and no  studies have found negative effects and  in addition to that they find these  positive effects for a lot less money so  school vouchers and charter schools  actually are getting the same results or  better for a much lower cost than  traditional public schools charter  schools and school vouchers are like  anything else there's good performers in  there there's bad performers but the key  difference with these kind of schools is  that they're held accountable because if  they don't succeed they lose their  funding because we're not bound to keep  that school open because of some  sentimental obligation that it's a  neighborhood school and they're  contractually obligated to serve their  students with school choice parents tend  to have much higher satisfaction they  tend to score very high on things like  safety the empirical evidence shows that  for middle schools and high schools  graduation rates are much better that  college attendance is much better so  while they may not always out score  traditional schools in terms of math and  reading there's a lot of quality of life  values that parents value that make  having the choice important to those  parents and is one of the reasons for  the popularity so for instance in New  York or LA when you have a successful  charter school you often have hundreds  of people waiting in line on waiting  lists to get into those schools and it's  because of academic performance but it's  also because of things that are harder  to quantify there's really no reason  that we should pick our k-12 schools  based on where we live instead because  of both technology and the idea that the  money should always follow the child we  can pick our schools based on the needs  of our child not on some other arbitrary  factor everything else we do we do based  on choice why not education
 

